# Multi-agent orchestration

One parent is the integration master. A planner classifies:

1. parent-owned sequential contracts/shared files;
2. disjoint writer packets;
3. read-only research/review/verification packets.

Use at most ten participants total and three concurrent writers. Parallelize only dependency-ready packets with frozen interfaces and disjoint write sets; otherwise state the exact dependency. Writers use isolated worktrees from a committed baseline, know other work exists, and never own shared dependency manifests, migrations, routes, or traceability unless explicitly assigned.

Every packet includes objective/non-goals, PRD IDs, accepted docs/ADRs, fixed commit, allowed/forbidden files, required tests, safety boundaries, and handoff format. Handoffs report commit/diff, files, commands/results, limitations, risks, and integration order.

The parent keeps the critical path moving, reviews every diff, integrates in dependency order, and reruns evidence. Security/UI reviewers inspect a fixed integrated commit; corrections return to an owner; the final verifier inspects the corrected immutable commit. No agent approves its own work. Missing external evidence remains a blocker to that claim, not permission to invent it.
